00:00:03.350 --> 00:00:05.349 align:start position:0%
the g20 summit began on saturday with a  plea from the host of the gathering  italian prime minister mario draghi we  must do all we can  to overcome our differences  and we must rekindle  the spirit that led to the creation of  this group coming into the summit  leaders from the world's major economies  agreed to a minimum global corporate tax  of 15  meant to reduce abuse of offshore  loopholes  the biggest issues though are proving  the most difficult  covid19 vaccine equity and taking  effective action on the climate crisis  a draft statement from the group shows  it's struggling to get member countries  to truly commit to capping global  warming at the 1.5 degree mark a  benchmark scientists have said is  required to avoid disaster  and this comes just days before the  climate change conference in glasgow  some key leaders opted to attend only  via remote video  chinese president xi jinping made only  vague comments about china opening up  further to the world but made no mention  of china's role as a major global  polluter russian president vladimir  putin had a more pointed request on  vaccine recognition russia's sputnik  covid19 vaccine has not been approved  for use by the u.s or the european union  in hopes of reviving the iran nuclear  deal u.s president joe biden met on the  sidelines with the leaders of the uk  france and germany they later issued a  statement warning of the dangers of a  nuclear-capable iran  outside the security perimeter  protesters gathered to call attention to  various causes  thousands of protesters from disparate  groups have come out into the street  here in rome on saturday  some are calling for action on the  climate crisis others are marching  against capitalism they all have one  message though and that's for world  leaders to address global inequity and  climate change now  we're marching today because we're  asking for more concrete policies on  climate change since the g20 is  happening right now in rome the summit  will continue on sunday people will be  watching closely to see if a closing  group statement will go far enough in  addressing climate change adam rainey al  jazeera rome
 

